    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 87515353091
    m_Localized: Destination
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 87519547392
    m_Localized: 'The process of protein synthesis occurs in the cytoplasm by ribosomes,
      which are either free or attached to the Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER)
      in eukaryotic cells, while in prokaryotes they are free in the cytoplasm. Since
      the genetic information is within the genes (inside the nucleus), they need
      to be copied and transported to the ribosomes in a process called transcription.
      The genes corresponding to a specific protein are present in a specific sequence
      of DNA. Thus, transcription of the information, in eukaryotes, occurs in the
      nucleus, meaning a gene will be copied from DNA, forming an intermediate molecule:
      messenger RNA (mRNA). DNA and RNA are molecules formed by nucleotides. Nucleotides
      consist of a sugar, ribose in RNA, hence called ribonucleotides, and deoxyribose
      in DNA, hence called deoxyribonucleotides, a phosphate, and a nitrogenous base.
      The following nitrogenous bases compose ribonucleotides: adenine (A), uracil
      (U), found exclusively in RNA molecules, cytosine (C), and guanine (G), while
      deoxyribonucleotides are formed by adenine (A), thymine (T), found exclusively
      in DNA molecules, cytosine (C), and guanine (G). Find the cell nucleus to initiate
      the transcription process and complete mission 1.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 87519547393
    m_Localized: 'You have correctly identified the nucleus, and transcription can
      now begin. RNA is formed by a single strand, from a DNA template strand. In
      this process, ribonucleotides pair with DNA nucleotides. There is a sequence
      of nucleotides in the DNA where the transcription process begins: TAC, so at
      the beginning of the mRNA there will be a sequence of AUG, called the start
      codon. The RNA polymerase enzyme will link one ribonucleotide to another, forming
      the mRNA. Once the entire gene is transcribed, the RNA strand is released from
      the DNA molecule. The mRNA is then detached from the DNA gene sequence when
      it encounters one of the sequences in DNA called "STOP": ATT, ATC, ACT, in
      other words, transcription stop codons. A complex of proteins, existing in
      the nucleus, will transport the mRNA through the nuclear membrane pores to
      the Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER) in the cytoplasm where the ribosomes
      are attached. Correctly add the bases that complement the DNA template strand
      to form an mRNA strand and forward the molecule to the ribosome (cytoplasm)
      to complete mission 2!'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 87519547394
    m_Localized: The process of translation is responsible for protein synthesis,
      meaning it will decode the mRNA, which has been transported to the cytoplasm
      and attached to the ribosomes, present in the Rough Endoplasmic Reticulum (RER).
      The ribosome will translate the information present in the mRNA, initiating
      the synthesis of the protein in question. For every 3 nitrogenous bases of
      the mRNA (which represent a codon), it adds the corresponding amino acid, which
      is attached to another type of RNA, the transfer RNA (tRNA). This correspondence
      between the 3 nitrogenous bases (codons) and the amino acids can be checked
      from the Genetic Code wheel, which is read from the center outwards, forming
      a path until it reaches the correct amino acid. The amino acids will be linked
      together through a specific chemical bond, called a peptide bond. Identify
      the corresponding amino acids with the codons (every 3 nitrogenous bases) of
      the mRNA and insert the correct name of the amino acid. After completing the
      mRNA strand, click on "process the protein" to complete mission 3!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 87519547395
    m_Localized: After the formation of the chain of amino acids, called the polypeptide
      chain or primary structure of the protein, it will still be rearranged into
      three-dimensional structures that refer to its spatial folding, necessary to
      exert their biological activities. Some proteins also undergo other structural
      modifications to become active. Other types of molecules can be attached to
      them, such as carbohydrates, forming glycoproteins; lipids, forming lipoproteins;
      or even metals, such as iron, copper, forming metalloproteins. The spatial
      structure comprises secondary structures, represented by alpha helices, beta
      strands, and loops, and the tertiary structure, which is the spatial arrangement
      that the secondary structures acquire when they "fold" around each other. In
      this form, many proteins are active and perform their biological functions,
      being called monomers. They can also be composed of two or more monomers, forming
      dimers, trimers, tetramers, and other polymeric forms, which are called quaternary
      structures. These processes are carried out by different enzymes and/or protein
      complexes present in the cytoplasm and are essential for the physiological
      function that the protein will perform. After all this processing, the protein
      becomes active or ready to be stored and used when necessary. Click "continue"
      to complete mission 4.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 87519547396
    m_Localized: After the synthesis and processing of proteins, many are stored
      as inactive pro-proteins in granules until they are secreted and/or activated
      when the body needs their actions, in its tissues or target locations. These
      locations can be cellular structures (such as the lysosome, nucleus, mitochondria),
      organs (such as the liver, kidneys), tissues, cells (such as melanocytes),
      etc. These proteins can act within the cell itself or be released directly
      into the bloodstream to act at specific sites. Answer the quiz about the destination
      of the chosen protein to complete the final mission of your journey!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
